anton@mips.complang.tuwien.ac.at (Anton Ertl) writes:
>Bernd Paysan has now built docker images with tags 1.0.0 and 1.0.1, so
>you can start, e.g., gforth-fast 1.0.1 with
>
>docker run -it --rm forthy42/gforth:1.0.1 gforth-fast
>
>One other aspect of Docker is: by default it does not allow the gforth
>engine running in the container to access files outside the container.
>
>I played around a little with the options --volume (-v), --mount, and
>--user (-u), but could not get the gforth in the container to write
>outside the container.

I have now written a shell script that calls docker with a gforth
engine and give it R/W access to your working directory and its
subdirectories on the same file system.

Download it from

https://www.complang.tuwien.ac.at/forth/gforth/docker.sh

Usage example:

./docker.sh gforth-fast onebench.fs

This includes the file onebench.fs that's inside the container unless
you happen to have a file onebench.fs in your working directory (the
one inside the container leaves Gforth when finished, so the container
also ends afterwards.  But if you have myfile.4th in your working
directory, you can say, e.g.,

./docker.sh gforth myfile.4th

to include it.

- anton
